WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:03.518
A yearly increment includes earlywood and latewood.

00:00:03.974 --> 00:00:07.855
A tree-ring boundary separates adjacent annual increments.

00:00:08.316 --> 00:00:11.796
A dark band alone is not the whole yearly increment.

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:15.862
Counting thin lines alone can miss important structure.

00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:21.720
Think of a ring as a band of wood with width and internal anatomical variation.

00:00:22.523 --> 00:00:25.978
Latewood of the older increment → earlywood of the next.

00:00:26.433 --> 00:00:29.782
Follow changes in lumen size and wall thickness.

00:00:30.236 --> 00:00:34.019
Check anatomy and continuity when colour is ambiguous.

00:00:34.473 --> 00:00:40.012
Use a consistent boundary convention and explain the anatomical feature supporting it.

00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:44.380
Check whether another measurement path gives a different view.

00:00:45.183 --> 00:00:46.500
False ring.

00:00:46.962 --> 00:00:51.291
Cell anatomy may change and then return within one growing season.

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:55.929
Check other radii and trees, and verify through crossdating.

00:00:56.383 --> 00:01:00.948
A boundary-like change within an increment need not indicate another year.

00:01:01.409 --> 00:01:05.792
Compare other radii and trees before counting it as annual.

00:01:06.595 --> 00:01:08.491
Locally absent ring.

00:01:08.953 --> 00:01:12.926
Visible ring counts can differ between radii of one tree.

00:01:13.387 --> 00:01:16.183
A single radius can miss an increment.

00:01:16.637 --> 00:01:20.209
An increment can become locally absent around a stem.

00:01:20.663 --> 00:01:25.812
Its absence along one radius does not mean that the calendar year is missing.

00:01:26.615 --> 00:01:28.978
Record observations first.

00:01:29.433 --> 00:01:33.940
Mark pore size, distribution, wall thickness and changes.

00:01:34.394 --> 00:01:35.940
Then interpret.

00:01:36.394 --> 00:01:39.992
Propose a wood type and boundaries with supporting evidence.

00:01:40.446 --> 00:01:42.453
Keep uncertainty visible.

00:01:42.907 --> 00:01:46.940
State which plane, magnification or sample would help.

00:01:47.394 --> 00:01:51.834
Pause and describe or sketch the feature used to identify a boundary.

00:01:52.288 --> 00:01:57.160
The exercise is about evidence rather than merely naming a wood type.

00:01:57.963 --> 00:02:01.526
Which wood type is represented by A, B and C?.

00:02:01.834 --> 00:02:05.071
Mark boundary candidates and give your evidence.

00:02:05.531 --> 00:02:07.906
These are schematic examples.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:13.992
Record visible openings, wall changes and arrangements before deciding which interpretation

00:02:14.005 --> 00:02:16.412
best fits the material.

00:02:17.215 --> 00:02:20.446
Compare candidates 1, 2 and 3.

00:02:20.901 --> 00:02:24.507
What additional evidence would resolve the interpretation?.

00:02:24.809 --> 00:02:27.855
Pause and justify the boundaries you select.

00:02:28.309 --> 00:02:33.217
Avoid treating every similar-looking line as another year without checking its anatomical

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:34.992
context.
